#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096439 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48176a87-796e-5637-9552-4f7fccdeee6a" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Two Vectors Using the Triangle Law"}] :question/id #uuid "96cb0487-40a4-5942-aa43-6ca61830bde0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4272" :question/problem "The vectors $\\textbf{a}$ and $\\textbf{b}$ are shown in the diagram below. Draw $\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/31/31170f98fe0a33f2f83a32240899f7274710d8735816cf4626585c411b984a67.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we draw the vector that is equal to $\\mathbf{b}$ and has its tail placed at the head of $\\mathbf{a}.$ \n\nNow, according to the triangle law of addition, the resultant \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis the vector that goes from the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ to the head of $\\textbf{b}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3af74d7b5cae7b1aa2b8f5ee5cfcb52b1a048316f214436ac953de0062c31f92.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096441 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096644 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db159911-4e9a-563e-9a3d-58d4e73dc862" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Two Opposite Vectors Using the Triangle Law"}] :question/id #uuid "6db52d38-e14d-5424-9b54-af258ad7551c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4274" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\overrightarrow{AB},$ shown below. What is the resultant of $\\overrightarrow{AB}+\\overrightarrow{BA}?$\n\n![](images/17/17531cbc767566b8c8d60536372001b2492d44456e818498e8edb2600471db49.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ to the head of $\\overrightarrow{BA}.$ Therefore, we obtain a new vector for which the tail and head are the same point, $A.$ \n\nThat vector is called the zero vector, and we write\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}+\\overrightarrow{BA} = \\overrightarrow{AA}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096443 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096646 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9ae0161-d016-5aef-8c11-020047c66bbd" :knowledge-point/title "Adding More Than Two Vectors Using the Triangle Law"}] :question/id #uuid "77d2bd5d-5f79-5204-8c65-395ef6414225" :question/math-academy-id "e-4276" :question/problem "The vectors $\\textbf{a},$ $\\textbf{b},$ and $\\textbf{c}$ are shown in the diagram below. Find $\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}-\\textbf{c}.$\n\n![](images/96/9665262608454d38d6a442715345cc014d40ca2e3d9a7cdf7c8cd0b8fc00f0d7.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the triangle law of addition to find \n\n$$\n\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}.\n$$\n\nThe resultant $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ is the vector that joins the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ and the head of $\\textbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/26/2643b94a575f1b2cd9967e530019b8b100928b2f9888aaf833dd84423fbe6e10.png)\n\nNow, we use the triangle law of addition to find \n\n$$\n(\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b})+(-\\textbf{c}).\n$$\n\nWe draw $-\\textbf{c}$ from the head of $\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}.$ So, we have the vector that goes from the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ to the head of $-\\textbf{c}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/d7/d77ad19b87fe8afba44663482ed6e8adfb2f251c4439c488ab7b3cde8b4a60ac.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096453 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "711d0e70-a248-5f93-a836-43bed5435d4f" :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting Two Vectors Using the Triangle Law"}] :question/id #uuid "5fade14b-9fa1-5777-afe3-c061907218f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4359" :question/problem "Consider the diagram that shows the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$. Draw $\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/a7/a7ed4f4f82c61679a81ce151335695f9ba226529aafe79a88700430e0594fecd.png)" :question/worked-solution "We interpret the subtraction\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nas the addition\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{b}),\n$$\n\nwhere $-\\mathbf{b}$ is the vector that has the same magnitude as $\\mathbf{b}$ but goes in the direction *opposite* of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFinally, we use the triangle law to add the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- We can start $\\mathbf{a}$ from an arbitrary point on the plane.\n\n- Next, we draw the vector that is equal to $\\mathbf{-b}$ and has its tail placed at the head of $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- Now, according to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ to the head of $-\\textbf{b}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d5ad4a8f48ccdaefcd9b6ae91ef533c6a77f5800e2adede362840700acd7785.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096644 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db159911-4e9a-563e-9a3d-58d4e73dc862" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Two Opposite Vectors Using the Triangle Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06edd154-245c-5e62-964d-008ce8a8084b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/564070d6b3db6d074045156b005b091ddcb55adca401f00256f6810995903863.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509706} {:answer/id #uuid "93cec5d4-cb50-5639-802a-c80bf37e3c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/90fe2ee6c4141f34a17ad7d22b9488893c544a4d998a9c4f2e704427e574c5da.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510000} {:answer/id #uuid "a87aa98e-0dfc-5bae-b156-f7898c0b5266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/65/65be75c62725c429579265b81d0c79686013c795cd3281e667c95d25feef3d64.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510046} {:answer/id #uuid "bd7938ed-c187-5415-b99b-a7e07ab8de2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f4/f469bc9939f4c9224a9f7a7b00698d6fb61f9798b6b09be985d1a6da2149022c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510087} {:answer/id #uuid "e019953e